            <ArticleTitle>Qualitative pain classification in hospice and pain therapy unit.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>604-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1049909111435810</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this study, we investigated patient's meaning attribution to pain in hospice and pain therapy unit, using a qualitative approach: narrative-based medicine. The data presented here were related to patients (n = 17) hospitalized in Rimini Hospice (Italy). These data were compared to those of patients (n = 21) with noncancer pain (control sample). The interviews were then analyzed according to the technique of thematic narrative analysis. The results of our research identified a differential process in pain processing in relationship to the meaning that the patient attributed to pain. The thematic analysis of the interviews allowed the inductive construction of a specific network of pain dimensions, which were summarized in &quot;the pain chronogram.&quot;</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
